the Turkish military is pushing deeper  into northern Syria this morning and  it's massive offensive against Kurdish  allies of the United States Turkey  accuses the Kurds of being terrorists a  human rights group says at least 16  people have been killed and 33 wounded  in the assault so far Turkish forces  attacked by land and air after US troops  in the region pulled back from their  positions near the Syrian border  President Trump who agreed to withdraw  those troops is now calling the Turkish  attack quote a bad idea Charlie d'Agata  is in northern Syria this morning he has  reported extensively from the region  well we just crossed that River from  Iraq to here in northern Syria it's a  crossing we've made several times in the  past year but this time it's different  the Kurdish forces who we've accompanied  in their fight against Isis are now  under attack themselves from a US and  NATO ally the Turkish military unleashed  an onslaught of artillery and air  strikes overnight targeting tilapia and  Syrian town of Rasul ie both recently  vacated by US forces and that's not all  the Turkish Defense Ministry say they  struck a hundred eighty so-called  terrorist targets across a hundred fifty  miles span that includes key cities of  Qamishli and kobani and they say a  ground offensive has begun today it is  already underway now commanders of the  Kurdish led Syrian democratic forces say  they've had to put a pause on operations  against Isis in order to face down this  Turkish invasion they also say the  offensive has jeopardized the security  of prisons housing thousands of Isis  prisoners risking a breakout or possibly  leading to an Isis resurgence now the  military action the past 24 hours has  sparked a mass exodus of thousands of  civilians who are trying to flee the  reach of Turkish artillery and air  strikes and  SDF has warned of a humanitarian  catastrophe if this Turkish offensive  continues at this intensity and I can  tell you we've spent years with these  SDF forces watching them from the  frontlines in their fight against Isis  men and women who've helped protect us  they say that they were broadsided by  what happened they feel abandoned and  betrayed Charlie d'Agata reporting from  northern Syria thank you
 

